# A host sweep said "all gates passed" about Rust it never compiled (2026-10-04)

`scripts/verify-gates.sh` reads its gate list out of `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh` and skips
the four gates that rebuild the workspace (`cargo check`, `cargo clippy`, `cargo test`, `GUI
protocol types`) unless `--with-build` is given. The skip has always been printed and
counted. It was not allowed to affect the verdict, so the last line of a run that had
skipped the only gate able to see a Rust problem read `all gates passed on the host`.

That hazard is written down in `docs/ci-test-debt.md`, in the section headed
「宿主门禁跳过的那四条腿，正是这一轮两个缺陷的藏身处」, whose 判据 tells the reader
to quote `N run, M skipped` rather than say "all green". Batch 11 met the same hazard the
same day anyway: the sweep was honest about the skip, the skip was read, and the batch was
pushed and went red regardless, because a sentence about how to read a log stops being a
control the moment there are logs to read.

This batch does two things. It fixes the finding that turned CI red, and it moves the rule
from prose into the runner: a run that skipped a build gate while the diff holds a file that
gate measures now ends `NOT COVERED` with exit 1 and names the files.

== 0. What CI said

Batch 11 is commit `45be2b00`. Its run, and batch 12's run on top of it, both failed the
same leg with the same error. Run 37206776703, `rust check / clippy / test`:

    $ gh run view 37206776703 --log-failed

The raw lines also carry a `rust check / clippy / testUNKNOWN STEP` job-name prefix and
colour escapes; the excerpt below is the same output with those two removed, so the
timestamps shown are the log's own.

