The platform legs are the two long ones (17m5s on windows-latest, 23m34s on macos-14,
measured in run 37201938718) and they never started, so the push bought nothing and lost
both.

== 1. What the host sweep had said about the same tree

Recorded in `docs/verification/verify-in-docker-entry-blindness-2026-10-04.log:386`, which
is a sweep of the same runner on this same day:

    all gates passed on the host (35 run, 4 skipped)

Four skipped is four build gates, always all four, because the skip is by exact label.
Nothing in that line is false and nothing in it is about the Rust in the tree: it is a
verdict about 35 gates, printed at the end of a run whose own banner said `build gates are
skipped`.

== 2. The finding, and what the skipped leg actually costs

The fix is the shape clippy asked for.
`crates/codegen/chaos-engine/tests/workspace_diff_undo.rs` around line 697, after `cargo
fmt`:

    // A preview read must not consume the undo point.
    assert!(
        error_code(&rollback(&engine, session_id, &proposal_id))
            .as_deref()
            .is_none(),
        "the undo point should still be there after a preview read"
    );

The assertion is the same one batch 11 shipped: a preview read must leave the undo point
rollback-able. `matches!(x, None)` and `x.is_none()` decide identically; what changed is
that the message now says which property failed, which `assert!(matches!(..))` could not
carry.

The whole leg, over the workspace and every target, on a warm cache:

    $ cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --locked -- -D warnings
        Checking xai-grok-pager-minimal v0.1.0 (/home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code/crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager-minimal)
        Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 2m 52s

Exit status 0, 93 `Checking`/`Compiling` lines, no warning left standing. That number
matters for the design below: the leg the fast loop exists to avoid is under three minutes
when `target/` is warm, so a rule that pushes a Rust change towards it is not expensive
advice.

The test target that could not compile runs and passes:

    $ cargo test -q -p chaos-engine --test workspace_diff_undo
    running 18 tests
    ..................
    test result: ok. 18 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.01s

== 3. The rule, as shipped

Three pieces in `scripts/verify-gates.sh`. Which files belong to the skipped gates:

    is_build_relevant_path() {
      case "$1" in
        *.rs | *.toml | *.lock) return 0 ;;
        apps/chaos-ui/src/generated/protocol.ts) return 0 ;;
        *) return 1 ;;
      esac
    }

The generated `.ts` file is the one input of the `GUI protocol types` gate that lives
outside the Rust tree; everything else that gate reads it generates. What the diff holds:

    changed_paths() {
      if [ -n "${VERIFY_GATES_CHANGED+x}" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "${VERIFY_GATES_CHANGED}"
        return 0
      fi
      git rev-parse --git-dir >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
      git diff --name-only HEAD 2>/dev/null
      local upstream
      upstream="$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref --symbolic-full-name '@{u}' 2>/dev/null)" || upstream=""
      if [ -n "${upstream}" ]; then
        git diff --name-only "${upstream}..HEAD" 2>/dev/null
      fi
      return 0
    }

`git diff --name-only HEAD` is the staged-plus-unstaged half, which is the shape batch 11
was in at sweep time; the `@{u}..HEAD` half is the same risk for someone who commits before
sweeping. Outside a git checkout, or with no upstream configured, the function says nothing
rather than inventing a diff, and the rule stays quiet.

And the verdict, reached only by a run that would otherwise have printed the green line:

    if [ "${unbuilt_count}" -gt 0 ] && [ "${allow_unbuilt}" = "no" ]; then
      echo "NOT COVERED  ${skipped} build gate(s) skipped while ${unbuilt_count} changed file(s) are theirs to measure:" >&2
      ...
      echo '              run: scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build' >&2
      return 1
    fi

`--allow-unbuilt-changes` is the escape hatch, and it does not buy silence: the pass line
grows `--allow-unbuilt-changes: N changed file(s) the skipped build gates would have
measured`.

== 4. The rule on the tree that motivated it

With `crates/codegen/chaos-engine/tests/workspace_diff_undo.rs` modified and uncommitted,
which is the batch-11 situation exactly:

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh

The run is 35 cheap gates plus the four skips, 311 s:

    PASS  docs localization
    PASS  localization guard self-tests
    PASS  secret scan
    SKIP  cargo check (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  cargo clippy (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  cargo test (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  GUI protocol types (build gate; --with-build runs it)

    NOT COVERED  4 build gate(s) skipped while 1 changed file(s) are theirs to measure:
                  crates/codegen/chaos-engine/tests/workspace_diff_undo.rs
                  run: scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build
                         (or --allow-unbuilt-changes when those files are not meant to be compiled here)
    EXIT=1

The two files the same batch had open under `scripts/` are not counted, which is the point
of `is_build_relevant_path`: the sweep stays cheap for the diffs the build gates cannot see.

== 5. Is the new rule measured, or just written?

The rule has six fixture runs and seven fixture assertions inside `scripts/verify-gates.sh
--self-test`. A passing self-test proves the fixtures agree with the runner, not that the
runner enforces anything, so the two load-bearing conditions were mutated one at a time. The
driver copies the file, checks the baseline is green and refuses to touch anything if it is
not, and restores with `cp` and compares with `cmp` on every path including a failure inside
the self-test.

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --self-test
    verify-gates self-test: 63/63 cases pass

Mutant A, `is_build_relevant_path` no longer claims any path is the build gates' business:

    *.rs | *.toml | *.lock) return 0 ;;      ->      *.rs | *.toml | *.lock) return 1 ;;

    == mutant: no changed path counts as the build gates' business
        exit=1  KILLED
        verify-gates self-test: 58 passed, 5 FAILED
        not ok a skipped build gate with changed Rust is not a pass (expected exit 1, got 0)
        not ok it says which gates went unmeasured and why (no line matching: ^NOT COVERED  1 build gate.s. skipped while 2 changed file.s.)
        not ok and it names the files rather than the count alone (no line matching: ^              crates/a.rs$)
        not ok pointing at the flag that would have covered them (no line matching: verify-gates.sh --with-build)
        not ok where the pass line still says what went unmeasured (no line matching: --allow-unbuilt-changes: 2 changed file.s. the skipped build gates would have measured)

Mutant B, the skip is never turned into a verdict:

    if [ "${unbuilt_count}" -gt 0 ] && ...    ->      if [ "${unbuilt_count}" -gt 9999 ] && ...

    == mutant: the skip is never turned into a verdict
        exit=1  KILLED
        verify-gates self-test: 58 passed, 5 FAILED
        not ok a skipped build gate with changed Rust is not a pass (expected exit 1, got 0)
        not ok it says which gates went unmeasured and why (no line matching: ^NOT COVERED  1 build gate.s. skipped while 2 changed file.s.)
        not ok and it names the files rather than the count alone (no line matching: ^              crates/a.rs$)
        not ok pointing at the flag that would have covered them (no line matching: verify-gates.sh --with-build)
        not ok the generated protocol types count as the GUI gate's (expected exit 1, got 0)

Five fixture assertions die under either mutant: the exit code, then the two message
assertions, then one that only mutant A reaches (the `--allow-unbuilt-changes` note) and one
that only mutant B reaches (the generated `.ts` counting as the GUI gate's). The ordering
inside each run matters -- the fixture asks whether the run refuses to pass before it asks
whether the run explains itself, so a mutant that only breaks the wording cannot hide behind
a green exit code.

Once the driver had put the file back:

    == restore byte-identical: cmp exit=0

== 6. The same runner, with the leg switched on

With the fix in place, the whole list including the four build gates:

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build
    PASS  docs localization
    PASS  localization guard self-tests
    PASS  secret scan
    PASS  cargo check
    PASS  cargo clippy
    PASS  cargo test
    PASS  GUI protocol types

    all gates passed on the host (39 run, 0 skipped)

39 gates in 1383 s. The `cargo clippy` line there is the command line CI runs, verbatim,
because the runner parses its list out of `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`'s `gates` array
instead of copying it -- which is the whole reason this green line and CI's leg can be
compared at all. 23 minutes is now the number a Rust batch weighs against the alternative
that produced this batch: one red CI run and two platform legs that never started.

== 7. What is still not covered

Three limits, none of them closed by this batch.

The rule reads paths, not content. A `.rs` file that was already in HEAD and is unmodified
cannot appear in the diff, so a workspace that does not compile for a reason unrelated to
the change (either a bad merge resolution that landed as a commit, or a feature flag the
sweep does not build) still passes. Only `cargo check` on the tree can see that, and
`--with-build` remains the only way to ask.

The four build gates are one platform. `cargo check --workspace --all-targets` on Linux
cannot see a `#[cfg(windows)]` block that stopped compiling; that is what the platform legs
are for, and they run on push, not locally.

`--allow-unbuilt-changes` is trust, not measurement. It is deliberately loud in the summary
for that reason, and the fixture asserts the note is printed rather than asserting the flag
exits 0.
